WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:05.629
We are a tag team and perhaps we need to be because this is

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:08.929
a controversial subject. And I come to you as an academic

00:00:08.949 --> 00:00:11.150
and I realize that that term has been used somewhat

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.740
pejoratively a few times today. Rather than talk about

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:20.149
theory I want to talk about common sense and start out with

00:00:20.169 --> 00:00:26.410
what we know and what do we know. Well we know that prudent

00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:32.639
and sensible banker does not loan someone $970,000 to buy a

00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:35.959
million dollar house. I think we can all agree on that. We

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:39.999
also know that if that person who is buying the house says

00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:44.359
but Mr. Banker you must allow me to put down only 30000

00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:49.539
since that is the only way I can get the ROE made Return on

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:54.559
Equity that I need on my investment. We know that the banker

00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:58.969
is going to say no. What else do we know? I think it was

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:02.299
pretty well rehearsed today. We know that in 2007 we had a

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.960
very highly leveraged very fragile financial sector. It was

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.849
basically 30000 down on a million dollar house. Now of

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:17.689
course we were told and we actually assume perhaps that the

00:01:17.709 --> 00:01:21.890
situation was not the same as a highly leveraged house

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:25.590
because banks and others had accurate risk models in

00:01:25.609 --> 00:01:29.930
effective ways to control risk, but we also know what

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:31.750
happened.

00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:36.239
So let me ask the question: would the situation have been

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:42.640
less dire had the financial sector been much less leveraged

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:45.709
and I assert to you that that is basically a rhetorical

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:51.179
question. It's not our position that high equity, low

00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:54.429
leverage sounds everything but I think we can all agree that

00:01:54.449 --> 00:01:58.269
this situation would have played out very differently. And

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:01.789
in fact this was what was eluded to before we can look back

00:02:01.809 --> 00:02:06.340
in history and we can see times when the bank sector was

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:09.659
much less highly leveraged, much more equity and things

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.859
still seem to function, which leads you to question why don'

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:19.019
t we have more equity and we have talked in various ways

00:02:19.039 --> 00:02:22.699
today about some of the advantages of having much greater

00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:27.189
equity. The go-down payments you might say. Systematic risk,

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:30.069
if certainly reduced, there is less chance of financial

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.299
crisis and the deadweight losses and we never should forget

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:36.719
the deadweight losses that are associated with the financial

00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:41.789
crisis which Daily Miles quantified in various ways here.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.049
Risk is privatized. We talked about the importance of having

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:51.279
the market basically governing how things are done rather

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:54.799
than leaving it to regulators. Well to the extent that

00:02:54.819 --> 00:02:59.219
insurance is being provided here it's by equity, not by the

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:03.079
government and so we can say that this is moving in the

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:06.299
direction that Kevin Warsh and others were saying of having

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:09.409
the market do it rather than the government. And the

00:03:09.429 --> 00:03:13.719
incentives to take socially unproductive risk are clearly

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:17.779
reduced at the margin if we increase equity. So social

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:21.769
benefits are quite large, which of course leads to the

00:03:21.789 --> 00:03:25.889
question why is there a controversy here, why are we up here

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:31.569
because if equity has so much to offer in terms of the

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.089
social benefits why don't we see more people calling for,

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:37.899
why is there such resistance. And of course the resistance

00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:41.939
is that it's expensive. Is it really expensive, that's the

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:44.079
question we want to ask.

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:47.099
And it's very, very, very important I put three verys in

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:51.479
there to distinguish between private and social and we have

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.559
sort of talked around the margins on this. Well let's be

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:58.909
very clear. What are the private cost to have more equity?

00:03:58.929 --> 00:04:04.669
Well the tax code, basically disfavors equity favors debt

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:10.819
financing. There is no doubt about that. The government

00:04:10.839 --> 00:04:14.569
makes debt cheap through implicit guarantees, the two big

00:04:14.589 --> 00:04:18.790
failed subsidies perhaps under priced deposit insurance,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.939
various things of that sort that favors debt over equity.

00:04:22.959 --> 00:04:27.519
And increasing equity as we will see it little bit later

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:33.009
when Peter talks mechanically reduces ROE and also reduces

00:04:33.029 --> 00:04:37.629
risk but if compensation is somehow tight, somewhat rigidly

00:04:37.649 --> 00:04:41.899
to ROE, this potentially can reduce someone's pay which gets

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:45.759
certain people's attention. What is the social cost, that's

00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:49.060
the real question here? And that's a big question mark. Let'

00:04:49.079 --> 00:04:54.280
s talk about a little bit more about these incentives. There

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.769
is a too-big-to-fail subsidy that we have talked about and

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:02.170
if a bank believes if the investors in the bank specifically

00:05:02.189 --> 00:05:05.470
the creditors believe that there is some potential for the

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.280
government to come in and give support that's going to lower

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.990
the required yield on that debt.

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:15.009
And you can do a very simple calculation to show that this

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:17.719
is a nontrivial effect. For instance if we just reduce the

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:22.350
average cost of liabilities by 2.5 basis points we are

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:26.459
building 3% equity that's a 1% increase in ROE. If we

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:30.000
increase or decrease that cost by 10 basis points again

00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:34.149
that's not all that much we get a 3% increase in ROE. So

00:05:34.169 --> 00:05:38.579
this definitely creates some incentives. Now is this

00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:43.610
hypothetical, is this academic theory? No, even Moody says

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:48.040
we talked earlier recognizes this and in fact gives two

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.129
different ratings - one based upon government support and

00:05:51.149 --> 00:05:54.639
one is a standalone. So let's talk about the incentives in

00:05:54.659 --> 00:05:57.240
banking very quickly. I am going to simplify banking a

00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:00.649
little bit here but not much, it's a spread business. You

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:05.280
will receive interest and then you pay interest but bankers

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.569
if they get this subsidy aren't really paying all the

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:10.660
interests that they should. So I think that subsidy is

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.449
basically given by the government that comes in the form of

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:17.079
less interest paid. And that's a minus to all of us in the

00:06:17.079 --> 00:06:19.180
room that aren't bankers.

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:23.029
And then of course we pay taxes, all of us, including the

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:27.209
banks but that's a benefit to the government. There is a

00:06:27.229 --> 00:06:30.339
question as to what's done with it. Now we divide this by

00:06:30.339 --> 00:06:34.180
equity we get required rate of Return on Equity or the

00:06:34.199 --> 00:06:37.779
Return on Equity here. And now let's look at what the

00:06:37.799 --> 00:06:42.139
incentives are. By taking that equity and making it smaller

00:06:42.159 --> 00:06:45.439
what happens? Well the first thing is I will pay a little

00:06:45.459 --> 00:06:50.509
bit more interest but that's increasing the subsidy and I

00:06:50.529 --> 00:06:54.939
will pay less taxes. So the bank is going to have a higher

00:06:54.959 --> 00:06:57.899
numerator here. The government has clearly lost it's minus

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:01.040
the subsidies plus the taxes both of those have gone down.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.100
And now we are dividing by a smaller number there and the

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:08.319
denominator that's going to make for higher ROE. So the

00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:11.790
incentives are very clear but let me also be very clear,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:16.069
just because bankers have an incentive to argue for

00:07:16.089 --> 00:07:19.439
economizing on equity for the very clear reasons that we

00:07:19.459 --> 00:07:23.420
just saw, that doesn't mean that they are wrong when they

00:07:23.439 --> 00:07:26.689
say that equity might be expensive but it does mean very

00:07:26.709 --> 00:07:31.199
strongly that we should be skeptical and we should demand

00:07:31.219 --> 00:07:34.750
that they support their claims with sound arguments.

00:07:34.769 --> 00:07:37.759
Now one thing that might be asserted is yes there are these

00:07:37.759 --> 00:07:42.120
subsidies but these subsidies are important because they do

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:45.259
beneficial things and they pass on some of these subsidies

00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:48.509
to those who are borrowing. Let's think about that just a

00:07:48.529 --> 00:07:54.129
little bit more. If we are basically giving tax in implicit

00:07:54.149 --> 00:07:58.149
government subsidies that are creating incentive for

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:01.980
leverage and high leverage that's bad because that creates

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.069
the systematic risk that we are all trying to avoid. And the

00:08:05.089 --> 00:08:07.769
question really is at the end of the day does that really

00:08:07.769 --> 00:08:13.129
lead to better loans and if it does let's do it directly,

00:08:13.149 --> 00:08:17.639
not to a mechanism that creates problems. So the big

00:08:17.659 --> 00:08:21.689
question here which we are going to look at in a little bit

00:08:21.709 --> 00:08:26.019
more detail here is are there social costs to bank equity.

00:08:26.039 --> 00:08:30.050
Now we started out looking at private costs but no, that's

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:33.049
not what we should look at. We should look at the social

00:08:33.049 --> 00:08:36.049
cost and be mindful of the social benefits that are very

00:08:36.069 --> 00:08:39.629
large and now there is a question as to what these social

00:08:39.649 --> 00:08:43.399
costs are. So what we are going to now do is turn to a very

00:08:43.399 --> 00:08:46.269
skeptical appraisal of the arguments that have been put

00:08:46.289 --> 00:08:50.049
forward for saying that that question right there is

00:08:50.069 --> 00:08:53.830
positive and large. I have made a small but I don't want to

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:54.939
prejudge things here is Peter.

00:08:54.939 --> 00:09:10.629
Peter: Thanks Paul and so obviously we were thinking about

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:15.840
today the tradeoff between capital requirements which we

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.960
have many have argued today it will reduce the probability

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.610
of financial crisis. On the other hand there is the issue of

00:09:24.629 --> 00:09:27.620
confidential higher borrowing cost and if that will slow

00:09:27.639 --> 00:09:31.460
economic growth. And so the real question is how do we trade

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.009
those off. We have seen some analysis today, here are some

00:09:35.029 --> 00:09:40.500
discussions about it. And what we are trying to do in our

00:09:40.519 --> 00:09:45.439
discussion is define that debate and get us to focus on what

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:49.379
the key issues are. Obviously we have heard a lot today

00:09:49.399 --> 00:09:51.899
about the increase in leverage that lead to the financial

00:09:51.899 --> 00:09:56.370
crisis and we think about sort of these deleveraging spirals

00:09:56.389 --> 00:10:02.939
that result from leverage if we have 3% equity and 1% the

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:08.279
client in asset value means that 30% reduction contraction

00:10:08.299 --> 00:10:10.500
of potential balance sheet contraction if we try to

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.919
recapitalize without raising new funds and so we get the

00:10:14.939 --> 00:10:17.830
problem of asset fire sales and liquidity market failure on

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.980
certain key bailout follow-up things that we observed in the

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:20.980
crisis.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:26.259
And so the question is can we address that with more capital

00:10:26.279 --> 00:10:32.240
is that a tool that regulator should feel confident

00:10:32.259 --> 00:10:37.230
utilizing in this context. And we are going back to David

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.639
Vineyard's point this morning where he argued that liquidity

00:10:40.659 --> 00:10:46.569
is costly to maintain but even more constantly if not

00:10:46.589 --> 00:10:50.710
maintained. I would say the same is true for capital in

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:55.199
terms of the benefit of maintaining capital is quite high

00:10:55.219 --> 00:10:58.600
and probably even higher given that capital adequacy and

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:05.110
solvency is a precursor to liquidity and yet I will argue

00:11:05.129 --> 00:11:08.429
that the costs are actually lower for maintaining adequate

00:11:08.449 --> 00:11:13.100
capital than for liquidity per se. But we know that there is

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:17.580
some cost for maintaining liquidity. For capital our

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.870
argument is very different and that's an important that we

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:23.639
think we would like regulators to understand in terms of

00:11:23.659 --> 00:11:27.379
being more flexible in their use of capital as a mechanism

00:11:27.399 --> 00:11:31.819
to increase safety in the banking sector.

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:34.799
So the question is equity too expensive and what I want to

00:11:34.799 --> 00:11:38.370
talk about in my section is sort of what are some of the

00:11:38.389 --> 00:11:40.889
arguments that have been used that you hear about why

00:11:40.889 --> 00:11:43.389
acquisition bank is expensive while we don't think there are

00:11:43.389 --> 00:11:48.179
right arguments and why our kind of question to

00:11:48.199 --> 00:11:52.860
practitioners who are concerned about these requirements is

00:11:52.879 --> 00:11:56.360
let's dig deeper into what might be the real cost because

00:11:56.379 --> 00:11:58.949
the ones that are pre-forth don't seem like the correct one.

00:11:58.949 --> 00:12:04.419
So first confusion so we are crowding out concern that

00:12:04.439 --> 00:12:07.889
increased capital requirements will force banks to reduce

00:12:07.909 --> 00:12:13.519
lending simply because it means they have to set aside some

00:12:13.539 --> 00:12:14.539
assets.

00:12:14.539 --> 00:12:19.090
And of course that's not what capital requirements are

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.799
about, it's not about setting aside any assets at the

00:12:21.799 --> 00:12:25.759
funding issue and it's about leverage so it's really

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:28.470
question of capital structure. So the idea of holding

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:31.559
capital is expensive is sort of a misnomer since we are not

00:12:31.579 --> 00:12:33.769
holding anything per se. It's not a question about how we

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:36.360
allocate assets.

00:12:36.379 --> 00:12:41.039
In terms of increasing capital there are different ways we

00:12:41.059 --> 00:12:43.759
can do that but importantly there is no mechanical

00:12:43.779 --> 00:12:48.740
requirement that we reduce lending through increased capital

00:12:48.759 --> 00:12:51.730
requirements. Of course that's one way it can happen if I

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.730
want to double the amount of capital that bank has. One way

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:59.340
to do it is to contract the assets and move from 10%

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.549
capital, 20% capital in that way. But an alternative way to

00:13:03.569 --> 00:13:09.539
do that is we could simply recapitalize some of the

00:13:09.559 --> 00:13:14.679
liabilities through an increased inequity. And we might

00:13:14.699 --> 00:13:17.850
argue well we don't necessarily want do that because some of

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:21.259
these liabilities like deposits are important as for the

00:13:21.279 --> 00:13:25.079
value creation of the banking system and services it

00:13:25.099 --> 00:13:28.740
provides. Well there is still a third way we can do which is

00:13:28.759 --> 00:13:31.470
an asset expansion, right we could simply raise new equity,

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:34.960
put new assets on the balance sheet, a third way to do that.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:40.159
So there is no mechanical reason sometimes argued that an

00:13:40.179 --> 00:13:44.809
increase in equity requirements are going to lead to a

00:13:44.829 --> 00:13:48.340
reduction in lending. Now of course we would say well it's

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.649
about the cost of these three different alternatives and

00:13:51.669 --> 00:13:54.309
there is some reason to think that banks might be pushed

00:13:54.309 --> 00:14:00.980
towards the former versus the latter in terms of raising new

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:06.819
equity, real benefit creditors. And it will help creditors

00:14:06.839 --> 00:14:10.370
and therefore recast that to every holder. And so one of the

00:14:10.389 --> 00:14:13.389
things from a regulatory standpoint to think about is either

00:14:13.409 --> 00:14:16.959
ways we can provide mechanisms that push banks in this

00:14:16.979 --> 00:14:23.079
direction versus this direction of course helping creditors

00:14:23.099 --> 00:14:28.870
is a way of attempting to get banks to pay for the cost of

00:14:28.889 --> 00:14:32.220
the risk they are taking as opposed to leaving it for

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.079
government bailouts and other subsidies from that point.

00:14:36.099 --> 00:14:39.049
So that's one thing to think about and one of the proposals

00:14:39.049 --> 00:14:43.799
on these lines is to suggest that we get banks to increase

00:14:43.819 --> 00:14:49.129
capital by looking at lagged asset base as opposed to

00:14:49.149 --> 00:14:52.049
allowing them to do it by asset contraction. It's certainly

00:14:52.069 --> 00:14:55.069
something to think about. But the key point is there is no

00:14:55.069 --> 00:14:58.009
mechanical need for increase in capital to lead to a

00:14:58.029 --> 00:14:59.860
reduction in lending.

00:14:59.879 --> 00:15:05.480
The second point is lower ROE which we heard, discussed this

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.240
morning that is a concern that you will hear from the banks

00:15:09.259 --> 00:15:14.590
that it's going to lower the returns to equity holders and

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:18.289
that statement is partially true but the point is that low

00:15:18.309 --> 00:15:21.569
ROE does not reduce firm value, doesn't necessarily reduce

00:15:21.589 --> 00:15:25.480
firm value and that lower expected ROE is appropriate given

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.389
the reduced risk with higher capital requirements. And in

00:15:28.409 --> 00:15:31.159
particular if we just look at what happens to ROE comparing

00:15:31.159 --> 00:15:37.439
a 10% capital with 20% capital it's true that ROE is lower

00:15:37.459 --> 00:15:41.439
in good times with a high capital but it's higher in bad

00:15:41.459 --> 00:15:43.899
times, right that capital providing that buffer in bad

00:15:43.919 --> 00:15:44.919
times.

00:15:44.919 --> 00:15:49.439
And so what we are sort of giving up in a sense up here for

00:15:49.459 --> 00:15:54.919
shareholders we are gaining down here and that if things are

00:15:54.939 --> 00:15:59.079
fairly priced and ignoring imperfections in the market these

00:15:59.099 --> 00:16:03.439
two should be equivalent in value terms from a shareholder's

00:16:03.459 --> 00:16:09.990
perspective. And so it doesn't reduce shareholder value per

00:16:10.009 --> 00:16:15.649
se. Now of course if the downside is already being if equity

00:16:15.669 --> 00:16:18.359
holders are not, don't need to worry about that downside

00:16:18.359 --> 00:16:20.769
because of other projections for government guarantees then

00:16:20.789 --> 00:16:23.579
they are arguing up subsidies again in this tradeoff and

00:16:23.599 --> 00:16:27.439
that would be an example of private incentive and not

00:16:27.459 --> 00:16:31.039
wanting to increase capital as we spoke of earlier.

00:16:31.059 --> 00:16:33.769
Performance evaluation, another issue obviously we think

00:16:33.789 --> 00:16:37.209
about ROE and I want to give another example of why ROE isn'

00:16:37.229 --> 00:16:40.709
t really a very good measure to think about the value

00:16:40.729 --> 00:16:45.329
created via the banks. We think of a manager, a fund manager

00:16:45.349 --> 00:16:49.250
with a 22% return and the one with a 20% return and who

00:16:49.269 --> 00:16:51.579
deserves higher compensation. Well obviously it's not enough

00:16:51.599 --> 00:16:55.399
information especially if I told you that the first one was

00:16:55.419 --> 00:17:00.419
taking a much riskier strategy and had much fewer assets

00:17:00.439 --> 00:17:02.579
under management.

00:17:02.599 --> 00:17:06.029
The point is that when we think about returns we really need

00:17:06.049 --> 00:17:08.629
to think about risk adjusted value added so the alpha

00:17:08.649 --> 00:17:12.210
generated time the assets under management or kind of

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:14.889
translate in the banking context we want to think about

00:17:14.909 --> 00:17:19.259
excess ROE above what's a fairer risk adjusted return given

00:17:19.279 --> 00:17:22.269
the risk they are taking times the amount of equity based

00:17:22.289 --> 00:17:25.629
that that's on and that measure is going to be invariant to

00:17:25.649 --> 00:17:29.410
leverage, again apps and other frictions and so forth. So

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.480
looking at ROE per se isn't really the right measure if we

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:35.139
want to think about value added.

00:17:35.159 --> 00:17:39.759
The third point on equity is expensive is simply that equity

00:17:39.779 --> 00:17:43.099
capital is much more costly so we got to increase the amount

00:17:43.119 --> 00:17:47.759
of equity capital that's going to raise the cost of doing

00:17:47.779 --> 00:17:52.670
business and that's going to be transferred to borrowers.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:55.690
And we heard this morning the cost of equity is a step

00:17:55.690 --> 00:18:01.920
function or might not move as we increase capital. And

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.779
really these arguments ignore the fact that we know the cost

00:18:04.799 --> 00:18:07.109
of equity will decline with higher capital and reduced risk

00:18:07.109 --> 00:18:10.889
offsetting entire cost. There is a fair amount of evidence

00:18:10.909 --> 00:18:14.049
for that empirical evidence it's also the basis of most

00:18:14.069 --> 00:18:16.899
models that the banks themselves use when thinking about a

00:18:16.919 --> 00:18:21.049
variety of products that they go ahead and market. So well

00:18:21.069 --> 00:18:24.159
that's got to be the case and so we really have to be

00:18:24.159 --> 00:18:26.809
arguing something about equity investors being irrational or

00:18:26.829 --> 00:18:29.190
there being some other supply constraints or other factors

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:31.069
in the market.

00:18:31.089 --> 00:18:34.039
So I want to kind of just do a simple thought experiment

00:18:34.039 --> 00:18:38.700
here. We have got assets and liabilities you think of the

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:43.629
asset side as sort of value creation unlike most standard

00:18:43.649 --> 00:18:47.240
industrial firms so value creation also happens on the

00:18:47.259 --> 00:18:50.829
liability side for banks in terms of deposits, deposit

00:18:50.849 --> 00:18:54.259
taking and transaction services and so forth. So we want to

00:18:54.279 --> 00:18:57.349
think of some of this as part of the underlying business of

00:18:57.369 --> 00:19:00.539
the bank. But there is a fair amount that's just of the

00:19:00.539 --> 00:19:05.129
funding piece and when I think about what happens if we

00:19:05.149 --> 00:19:08.369
separate some of that and we take some of that funding and

00:19:08.389 --> 00:19:11.779
replace it rather than being highly leveraged equity in

00:19:11.799 --> 00:19:16.059
place. And the point of course is then in the rational

00:19:16.079 --> 00:19:20.740
market the cost of this larger equity piece should be the

00:19:20.759 --> 00:19:27.119
blended cost of an equity that we had before. This would be

00:19:27.139 --> 00:19:30.619
again just a standard argument we would be making any sort

00:19:30.639 --> 00:19:32.829
of portfolio analysis.

00:19:32.849 --> 00:19:35.609
And therefore that it shouldn't raise the weighted average

00:19:35.629 --> 00:19:41.579
cost of capital for the bank. That shareholder should

00:19:41.599 --> 00:19:44.629
understand this. If they don't then something must be

00:19:44.649 --> 00:19:47.220
fundamentally wrong in the market. Again, either there is

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:49.539
some irrationality or supply constraints or other factors.

00:19:49.559 --> 00:19:52.419
Now if you like one thing you could think about is well

00:19:52.419 --> 00:19:56.829
suppose we did that and suppose we were concerned that this

00:19:56.849 --> 00:19:59.750
cost of equity reduction would not occur, one could think

00:19:59.769 --> 00:20:03.680
about separating the leverage kind of from the bank entity

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:06.930
itself so we could imagine taking this equity, pulling it

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:23.190
and then creating products that replicate these payoffs that

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.710
could be sold to investors but separate from the bank

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:28.210
activity itself. So even if there are ultimately losses on

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:30.710
some of these products to equity link type products they

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:33.960
don't put the bank in any sort of the stress. So we could

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.460
imagine separating those and the real question is what is

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:39.460
the cost from doing this in fact this might be even more

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.960
flexible in terms of if investors prefer more leverage or

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:44.210
less leverage we could get the optimal mix determines

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.710
separately from the bank's activity itself.

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:48.210
So that's, let me leave it there and turn over

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.460
[inaudible] I think the key questions are we talked about

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.710
taxes and government guarantees as private benefits and to

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:56.750
the banking sector but not about social benefits. We talked

00:20:56.769 --> 00:20:59.279
about some of the key arguments that you hear for a why

00:20:59.279 --> 00:21:02.339
equity is costly and said that they don't really hold up so

00:21:02.339 --> 00:21:06.029
the key question I think for practitioners and for the

00:21:06.049 --> 00:21:08.549
researchers think about well if there are costs what are

00:21:08.549 --> 00:21:12.509
they and let's get down and define it precisely so we can

00:21:12.529 --> 00:21:15.029
measure them and so we can make sure whatever policy

00:21:15.029 --> 00:21:17.069
prescriptions we have are directed towards what those true

00:21:17.069 --> 00:21:20.470
costs are.

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:21.490
[Informal Talk]

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:31.399
So the key question that we have here is high leverage

00:21:31.419 --> 00:21:34.710
somehow a function that's something inherent in the banking.

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:38.440
This comes with the territory of banking and got to there

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:42.259
because that's the way it is, it's a debt business, it's a

00:21:42.279 --> 00:21:45.009
spread business, it's a leverage business. Our answer to

00:21:45.029 --> 00:21:49.639
this question is that we have looked at all of the arguments

00:21:49.659 --> 00:21:52.349
that were made and actually some that are not yet in our

00:21:52.349 --> 00:21:56.769
paper that are also made. And so far in about a year or more

00:21:56.789 --> 00:22:00.059
looking at it we actually have not found anything that would

00:22:00.079 --> 00:22:05.980
convince us that high leverage is necessary for banks to

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.750
function.

00:22:08.769 --> 00:22:13.909
It is true that some debts of the bank as was mentioned is

00:22:13.929 --> 00:22:17.389
part of their business but that is not the same. The amount

00:22:17.409 --> 00:22:22.049
of debt is not the same as the amount of leverage. So it

00:22:22.069 --> 00:22:25.299
incurred about large social cost and we do not see any

00:22:25.319 --> 00:22:31.089
benefit from the high leverage socially. Everything about

00:22:31.109 --> 00:22:33.879
the value the banks create can be done with less leverage.

00:22:33.899 --> 00:22:37.529
There is absolutely nothing we can see in the economics of

00:22:37.549 --> 00:22:41.849
it that would tell you that 25% equity on total balance

00:22:41.869 --> 00:22:46.509
sheet should be unthinkable for banks. If somebody thinks

00:22:46.529 --> 00:22:50.359
that that is a terrible-horrible thing we haven't seen why

00:22:50.379 --> 00:22:55.960
yet. In particular this balance sheet C if you were to think

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:59.039
about and I do not mean to suggest that bank should grow

00:22:59.059 --> 00:23:02.230
even larger this is just a way to think about this because I

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:05.690
think once they have more equity funding we will see the

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:10.089
natural size of the banking sector and of individual banks

00:23:10.109 --> 00:23:14.169
more organically is whether adding equity is somehow

00:23:14.189 --> 00:23:18.079
expensive and in what sense is it expensive. In fact, I

00:23:18.099 --> 00:23:22.119
would argue, we argue that it's not only not expensive, it's

00:23:22.139 --> 00:23:26.659
a social bargain, only good things happen when you do that

00:23:26.679 --> 00:23:31.149
for what anyway. In other words, as far as I can see from

00:23:31.169 --> 00:23:34.419
where we are right now or we can see it's a benefit to

00:23:34.419 --> 00:23:36.919
increasing equity, we do not see cost, certainly not high

00:23:36.919 --> 00:23:40.169
cost. But we didn't say our equity is not costly, we say it'

00:23:40.169 --> 00:23:46.629
s not expensive, expensive is in different means, expensive.

00:23:46.649 --> 00:23:52.569
So you could say that we need safety net expansion and I

00:23:52.589 --> 00:23:55.839
think we already agreed on this form that that is not a good

00:23:55.839 --> 00:23:59.169
thing. The safety net was created to prevent inefficient

00:23:59.189 --> 00:24:05.930
bank run in FDIC before that, by the way, banks had in the

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:09.589
US not even limited liability. So banks in the US until the

00:24:09.609 --> 00:24:15.190
1930s-40s in many states, I had double triple and unlimited

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.940
liability and the liability is the downside of the note

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:22.220
every time was warned by equity, except that in the question

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:24.990
there was a lot of personal bankruptcy and it was decided

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.740
there were a lot of inefficient runs and when banks were

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:30.240
very small and not diversified there were runs, even though

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:33.490
there was a lot of equity. So it's not like there were runs

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:36.490
for different reasons and so we have got a better system and

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:39.240
the system has become more leveraged as we saw. But the

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:41.240
safety net has expanded as a bank of

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:43.490
[inaudible] has shown and the question is whether we have

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:46.139
sort of too much of it.

00:24:46.159 --> 00:24:50.960
So to expand it, without giving a free gift to the bank is

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:53.839
to charge for it which would mean some kind of a bailout

00:24:53.839 --> 00:24:56.339
fund but that's very, very difficult to price and already

00:24:56.339 --> 00:24:59.589
FDIC is hard enough to price. So it's not, if we can avoid

00:24:59.589 --> 00:25:02.889
it, that's not the way to just insure everything. It would

00:25:02.909 --> 00:25:03.930
be very hard and

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:06.970
[inaudible] is always there because there is always basics

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:10.740
that are trying to take advantage of those guarantees. So we

00:25:10.759 --> 00:25:14.009
do not recommend that if it's possible to create a little

00:25:14.029 --> 00:25:17.819
more self-insurance and self-insurance is precisely equity.

00:25:17.839 --> 00:25:22.259
Equity means that the private sector bears a lot more of the

00:25:22.279 --> 00:25:24.779
downside and so it's a question of whether their economies

00:25:24.779 --> 00:25:29.210
can spend that, can stand to have 20% of risk capital in

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:33.599
economy a backup of the bank's liability instead of some

00:25:33.619 --> 00:25:36.470
other system.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:40.220
We have their presentation about loss absorbing debt and

00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:43.710
this is sometimes people think of contingent capital and

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:48.429
bail-enabled debt as a substitute for equity. We believe

00:25:48.449 --> 00:25:53.440
that it's a poor substitute. In the crisis, no hybrid

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:58.049
suffered any losses. The idea here is to conserve on equity

00:25:58.069 --> 00:26:00.919
and since we do not believe equity is expensive, we do not

00:26:00.919 --> 00:26:05.669
see a reason to conserve on equity and create magical

00:26:05.689 --> 00:26:08.829
complicated ways to get the debt, to become equity because

00:26:08.849 --> 00:26:13.509
then we have to ask why it was debt in the first place which

00:26:13.529 --> 00:26:16.779
is like long-term debt or others, why was it issued as debt

00:26:16.779 --> 00:26:20.089
to begin with. What was it doing as a debt because it's not

00:26:20.089 --> 00:26:24.409
the secure debt, it's not the deposits that are going to be

00:26:24.429 --> 00:26:27.519
inflicted that there is unsecured subordinated debt, why is

00:26:27.539 --> 00:26:32.609
it there instead of being equity so I am going to ask that

00:26:32.629 --> 00:26:33.629
question.

00:26:33.629 --> 00:26:36.879
Bail-in is of course sort of can be thought of as part of

00:26:36.879 --> 00:26:40.429
resolution and as a way to sort of preempt the bankruptcy,

00:26:40.449 --> 00:26:43.579
the laws of the bankruptcy what happens when you default.

00:26:43.599 --> 00:26:46.669
And obviously, we want a better system than bankruptcy laws

00:26:46.689 --> 00:26:49.649
so I completely agree with that. And in that context,

00:26:49.669 --> 00:26:52.579
obviously, we should be thinking about whatever we can do

00:26:52.599 --> 00:26:57.240
across countries and all these things. But it is not a

00:26:57.259 --> 00:27:01.859
substitute for pushing equity requirements way higher and we

00:27:01.879 --> 00:27:05.509
would even say way higher than David Mild's recommend.

00:27:05.529 --> 00:27:12.319
And so why bankers agree for to cocos? Well, Barclays told

00:27:12.339 --> 00:27:15.339
us they want cocos so that their ROE will not be affected,

00:27:15.339 --> 00:27:19.839
that is not a valid reason. Taking advantage of taxes, tax

00:27:19.859 --> 00:27:23.029
subsidies to the extent it is tax deductible is also not a

00:27:23.049 --> 00:27:27.710
social cost as we explained. So our feel is that if you

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:30.899
compare if whatever you were going to make contingent

00:27:30.919 --> 00:27:33.419
capital at that time that you issue it as contingent

00:27:33.419 --> 00:27:39.710
capital, you instead make it equity. Then when the assets go

00:27:39.730 --> 00:27:45.450
down, obviously, we don't want bailout so none of us kind of

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:49.369
want that. If contingent capital was going to bring the

00:27:49.389 --> 00:27:53.730
equity that you need, if it was issued as equity, you would

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:57.659
also have it there. So it's not about recapitalization it's

00:27:57.679 --> 00:28:01.299
about capitalization. So whatever contingent capital was

00:28:01.319 --> 00:28:04.569
going to do for you, equity can do for you and somebody is

00:28:04.569 --> 00:28:07.319
going to have to explain why contingent capital then is not

00:28:07.319 --> 00:28:09.569
better than debt but why it's better than equity.

00:28:09.569 --> 00:28:14.829
There are other things that are said some of them are

00:28:14.849 --> 00:28:18.619
academics. They are not logical policies or confusions

00:28:18.639 --> 00:28:22.240
between private and social costs and benefits. But we have

00:28:22.259 --> 00:28:26.539
theories that say debt contracts have discipline. Well,

00:28:26.559 --> 00:28:31.679
discipline as we already heard does not work when there is a

00:28:31.699 --> 00:28:35.259
bailout. So anything that guaranteed is not going to do any

00:28:35.279 --> 00:28:38.089
discipline for you, we can agree on that. The threat to run

00:28:38.089 --> 00:28:40.720
is not going to discipline when it's not a credible threat,

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:44.139
the threat to default doesn't do anything, if there is a

00:28:44.159 --> 00:28:50.079
bailout or if you can recapitalize immediately so there

00:28:50.099 --> 00:28:53.779
needs to be a real threat there and it's not there. And the

00:28:53.799 --> 00:28:57.500
question also is even if you were to say that debt can

00:28:57.519 --> 00:28:59.919
discipline and debt does discipline in many models which

00:28:59.939 --> 00:29:02.639
explains why the bank itself does not take equity position

00:29:02.659 --> 00:29:05.569
in the small businesses that it runs so debt is an optimal

00:29:05.569 --> 00:29:09.809
contract for pay me and then I wanted to worry about it

00:29:09.829 --> 00:29:13.329
until you don't pay me and then I will worry about it at a

00:29:13.329 --> 00:29:17.299
cost. So that's kind of optimal contracting for small people

00:29:17.319 --> 00:29:20.210
but not for the banks necessarily.

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:23.559
Even if it was providing some discipline the question is do

00:29:23.579 --> 00:29:26.789
we need it at the cost, at the social cost that it involves?

00:29:26.789 --> 00:29:29.539
Can we do governance and how do other companies in the

00:29:29.539 --> 00:29:33.279
economy do their governing when they have 80%, 90%, 100%

00:29:33.299 --> 00:29:37.129
equity? How do they actually discipline their managers? Do

00:29:37.149 --> 00:29:39.730
they need high leverage and if they don't and if they choose

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:43.480
not to then how do they do it and can banks also do it? Can

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:46.230
they get a better contract with the managers, can they get

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.849
the managers preferred, can they do anything that would not

00:29:48.869 --> 00:29:51.589
create this cost on the economy?

00:29:51.609 --> 00:29:56.669
So then people talk about assurance costs. Here there are

00:29:56.689 --> 00:30:01.700
two parts to it, one is the sort of we have buzzword for it,

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.470
it's call Myers and Majluf, it's an insurance cost about the

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:08.720
sigma associated with issuing equity or withholding

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:15.519
dividends and yes, but that is not a reason to not move from

00:30:15.539 --> 00:30:18.289
the level of capital that we have now to another level. That

00:30:18.289 --> 00:30:22.039
is more of a transition thing, but once you are there, any

00:30:22.059 --> 00:30:24.929
friction of this sort you have is actually lower. So

00:30:24.949 --> 00:30:28.339
whatever you are talking about being at 5% versus being at

00:30:28.359 --> 00:30:34.039
20% equity is better even on that front because you have

00:30:34.059 --> 00:30:38.609
left need to erase equities so you are not in the market as

00:30:38.629 --> 00:30:42.189
much because you have retained earnings as a view. And this

00:30:42.209 --> 00:30:45.240
sigma also is not a social cost and can be in helped by

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:49.009
removing discussion which was already mentioned here, so

00:30:49.029 --> 00:30:51.609
that there was no sigma. Just like thought was given to

00:30:51.609 --> 00:30:57.299
banks in sitting in a room and not allowed to spook anybody

00:30:57.319 --> 00:30:59.200
by taking or not taking the money.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.839
The big picture, if you want to look at the big picture is

00:31:03.859 --> 00:31:06.859
it's sort of a big picture of balance sheet scene and the

00:31:06.859 --> 00:31:09.609
big picture says when people ask where is all this equity

00:31:09.609 --> 00:31:13.059
going to come from? Well, equity comes from end investors in

00:31:13.079 --> 00:31:16.419
the end for the entire economy. So if you look at the big

00:31:16.439 --> 00:31:21.009
picture here, then what we have in one case of highly

00:31:21.029 --> 00:31:23.799
leveraged banks is we have three kinds of assets, some

00:31:23.819 --> 00:31:46.389
assets are held directly, that's class B.

00:31:46.409 --> 00:31:47.409
[Informal Talk]

00:31:47.409 --> 00:31:51.169
So in here we have a highly leveraged bank or banking system

00:31:51.189 --> 00:31:54.189
and some of the assets in the economy are held through that,

00:31:54.199 --> 00:31:56.449
some are held directly, some are held through other

00:31:56.449 --> 00:31:59.379
intermediaries. Now if I move to my balance sheet seesaw it

00:31:59.399 --> 00:32:02.149
didn't touch whatever the bank is doing, I didn't touch all

00:32:02.149 --> 00:32:05.049
the loans that are wonderful and I didn't touch all the

00:32:05.049 --> 00:32:08.799
deposits that everybody needs for liquidity or other things

00:32:08.819 --> 00:32:11.709
that are there. I didn't touch. I just did my balance sheet

00:32:11.709 --> 00:32:14.750
fee just for their sake of showing you that I don't need to

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:18.000
touch anything in the economy. I am only regrouping claims

00:32:18.019 --> 00:32:21.569
in the economy. So in this balance sheet, right in this

00:32:21.589 --> 00:32:27.329
picture right here, A, B and C are still there and I don't

00:32:27.349 --> 00:32:30.349
even mind if the banks have more assets in the economy. Let

00:32:30.349 --> 00:32:32.599
them buy more stuff and back up their liabilities.

00:32:32.599 --> 00:32:38.039
It aligns something better when that happens so even if you

00:32:38.059 --> 00:32:40.809
were to tell me theoretically and I don't believe that they

00:32:40.809 --> 00:32:44.309
will grow this way but I will even say I don't care if they

00:32:44.309 --> 00:32:47.089
go as long as it's with equity then nothing really

00:32:47.109 --> 00:32:52.389
productive changes in the economy and because end investors

00:32:52.409 --> 00:32:55.359
didn't hold everything that they held before. It is the case

00:32:55.359 --> 00:33:00.569
though that if the government was going to bail out these

00:33:00.589 --> 00:33:05.509
banks then it's not bailing as much out these banks over

00:33:05.529 --> 00:33:09.679
here. But we think there is capacity to move to there.

00:33:09.699 --> 00:33:15.519
Okay so basically more equity has benefits because highly

00:33:15.539 --> 00:33:18.319
leveraged firms in general, but especially, highly leveraged

00:33:18.339 --> 00:33:21.179
firm that have implicit guarantees have incentives that

00:33:21.199 --> 00:33:24.730
explicitly works. This is a problem that basically makes

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:29.359
firms stay away from high leverage because that will

00:33:29.379 --> 00:33:32.179
recognize that they will end up taking, such a person will

00:33:32.199 --> 00:33:34.889
require a return for that or will he require a lot of

00:33:34.889 --> 00:33:38.849
covenants to protect from that. So that in all companies is

00:33:38.869 --> 00:33:44.449
going to become a little onerous on the firm which why many

00:33:44.469 --> 00:33:48.109
firms don't want to leverage. But the bank debt holders do

00:33:48.129 --> 00:33:51.569
not impose such covenants and other things.

00:33:51.589 --> 00:33:56.149
The big other issue hanging here is that over hang, and this

00:33:56.169 --> 00:33:59.129
is a reason actually that banks are really reluctant or

00:33:59.149 --> 00:34:02.879
unable to raise funds or unwilling to raise funds for new

00:34:02.899 --> 00:34:05.579
loans when they already have highly leveraged. So when you

00:34:05.599 --> 00:34:09.829
have a lot of debts already in place, you are impaired in

00:34:09.849 --> 00:34:14.289
functioning. And that is the reason that we had a credit

00:34:14.309 --> 00:34:16.750
freeze. The reason for credit reduction is not too much

00:34:16.750 --> 00:34:20.599
equity, it's too much debt and I will talk some more about

00:34:20.619 --> 00:34:23.960
lending if I have the time.

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:26.730
We have level playing field up ahead with one of my

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:31.980
coauthors about this, so one has to think about who is

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:35.000
backing up. As long as there are no subsidies, it's good to

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:40.360
have competition. The unregulated shadows, is basically not

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:44.319
an argument for anything because we had shadow banking with

00:34:44.339 --> 00:34:46.799
the low capital requirements. So if we want to flow our

00:34:46.799 --> 00:34:49.799
hands in the air, and say we can't enforce anything, that we

00:34:49.799 --> 00:34:52.549
might as well no tax because of tax loopholes. In other

00:34:52.549 --> 00:34:54.889
words that's just a defeatist argument, can you or can't you

00:34:54.889 --> 00:34:58.539
regulate, is the question to that, that there would be

00:34:58.559 --> 00:35:01.340
enforcement issues as clear with any regulation. And there

00:35:01.360 --> 00:35:04.679
were enforcement issues before. So it's not like this

00:35:04.699 --> 00:35:09.269
problem is going to be new or necessarily worth because it's

00:35:09.289 --> 00:35:12.289
always going to be there. It's the same problem with low and

00:35:12.289 --> 00:35:14.659
high capital requirements you need to figure out how to do

00:35:14.659 --> 00:35:16.119
it.

00:35:16.139 --> 00:35:23.239
And it's kind of not, it's an enforcement issue and it is a

00:35:23.259 --> 00:35:26.349
challenge to enforce these regulations. But we just have to

00:35:26.369 --> 00:35:29.869
figure out what we want to do. And then we have to try to

00:35:29.869 --> 00:35:32.619
challenge it. Will there be, might there be a lending order

00:35:32.619 --> 00:35:34.869
of contraction, if you really think about this question.

00:35:34.869 --> 00:35:37.400
There is, first of all the possibility, just the possibility

00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:40.599
that it actually is not true. These great loans are going to

00:35:40.619 --> 00:35:44.019
lie there and not being taken. Well in New York these days,

00:35:44.039 --> 00:35:48.110
it's my co-lenders that lend because banks kind of don't

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:50.880
feel like it's too much work to check credit worthiness it

00:35:50.880 --> 00:35:54.849
seems. But in the Option expensing Debate we heard that the

00:35:54.869 --> 00:35:57.599
economy will shrink and that didn't happen when options

00:35:57.619 --> 00:36:00.159
became expense, so just because people said something doesn'

00:36:00.179 --> 00:36:04.039
t necessarily make it true. But if you think about whether

00:36:04.059 --> 00:36:06.889
credit will go down, well may be some credit should go down.

00:36:06.889 --> 00:36:10.139
We think we have a credit bubble and we can do too much

00:36:10.139 --> 00:36:12.889
lending. So not all lending is good lending, we want good

00:36:12.889 --> 00:36:14.139
lending, not just any lending.

00:36:14.139 --> 00:36:16.509
So if some lending doesn't happen then might be just as

00:36:16.509 --> 00:36:20.489
well. We don't know what the two economic costs should be.

00:36:20.509 --> 00:36:23.539
The risk rate gives distorted incentives. This is, it was

00:36:23.559 --> 00:36:26.219
discussed at lunch time and one of my coauthors is very

00:36:26.219 --> 00:36:28.250
strong and strongly disillusioned with risk rates. So

00:36:28.269 --> 00:36:31.460
Raphael presented the view, the risk rates could be

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:34.819
distorted and one of the immediate ways to satisfy capital

00:36:34.839 --> 00:36:37.679
requirements based on risk weighted assets to move to low

00:36:37.699 --> 00:36:40.449
risk weighted assets. I would only point out about risk

00:36:40.469 --> 00:36:43.789
weights that Greek Sovereign that has zero risk weight and

00:36:43.809 --> 00:36:47.949
pays 20% right now. And that sort of rests the case and AAA

00:36:47.969 --> 00:36:50.719
Securities also has zero risk rates and turnout to be quite

00:36:50.719 --> 00:36:53.339
systemic. So risk rates do not give you a measure and we

00:36:53.339 --> 00:36:55.250
already had that covered.

00:36:55.269 --> 00:36:58.539
The debt overhang itself where we are right now would make

00:36:58.559 --> 00:37:02.420
you not lend. And so that by itself is because we are

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:06.639
already in a bad place and not because of where we want to

00:37:06.659 --> 00:37:10.409
go. Where we want to go is to be in a place where there won'

00:37:10.409 --> 00:37:13.409
t be as much of that. If lending spreadsheet increase may be

00:37:13.409 --> 00:37:15.909
because of the subsidies that are removed and that there

00:37:15.909 --> 00:37:20.739
would be some little bit of a margin there, but it depends

00:37:20.759 --> 00:37:23.420
what we want to subsidize, do we want to subsidize lending

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:26.529
or do we want to subsidize the leverage of the banks so that

00:37:26.529 --> 00:37:29.029
they can pass it on somehow, it's very inefficient way.

00:37:29.029 --> 00:37:34.049
So the summary is that we do not have numbers, okay, people

00:37:34.069 --> 00:37:38.250
press us on the numbers because we don't begin to have an

00:37:38.269 --> 00:37:42.440
economic theory of the costs where we are right now. So

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:45.980
David presented some cost, so we can argue even with David

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:51.920
on exactly what those costs are. But we look at the recent

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:57.319
30%. We just don't see where the costs come in. I don't see

00:37:57.339 --> 00:38:03.250
the big as Debbie pointed out miles, it's not clear, it's

00:38:03.269 --> 00:38:08.159
debt sensitive once you get to it. So it's not going to be a

00:38:08.179 --> 00:38:10.779
decimal point because I cannot even quantify some of these

00:38:10.799 --> 00:38:14.440
other benefits that I see, and the costs that I can barely

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:17.429
find, so it's going to be very hard to make any kind of on

00:38:17.429 --> 00:38:20.190
the same scale, from where we are right now or even from

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:21.630
Basel.

00:38:21.650 --> 00:38:24.009
Requirements should not be one number, I want to mention

00:38:24.009 --> 00:38:27.420
that because the best line on this is that the child's good

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:31.339
hard line on the taxi cab in the station. So if you have

00:38:31.359 --> 00:38:34.109
regulation that says if there should be a taxi cab waiting

00:38:34.109 --> 00:38:37.529
at the train station at all times then when you get to the

00:38:37.549 --> 00:38:40.389
train station at 2 in the morning the taxi cab cannot take

00:38:40.389 --> 00:38:42.599
you because then there won't be a taxi cab in the train

00:38:42.599 --> 00:38:45.730
station. So the capital requirements cannot be one number

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:48.889
and we see what happens when people just be leveraged to a

00:38:48.909 --> 00:38:52.269
number, where you need to draw down on it, you must have a

00:38:52.289 --> 00:38:56.509
range. So the buffer in Basel in principle is a big

00:38:56.529 --> 00:38:59.529
breakthrough and it is a good thing. There has to be a

00:38:59.529 --> 00:39:02.279
range, but the range, and within their range what Basel does

00:39:02.279 --> 00:39:06.429
is it restricts bid and which is exactly the kind of sort of

00:39:06.449 --> 00:39:09.339
graduated responses that you want to have, is sort of to

00:39:09.359 --> 00:39:13.429
kind of stop capital from leaking out which we unfortunately

00:39:13.449 --> 00:39:17.619
allow it to happen through the crisis. And then maintain the

00:39:17.639 --> 00:39:20.670
leverage that its regulators have which is equity insurance

00:39:20.690 --> 00:39:21.690
and equity payouts.

00:39:21.690 --> 00:39:26.079
In particular through transition all you need to say and

00:39:26.099 --> 00:39:29.869
then you can't even tell me about issuance cost is we are

00:39:29.889 --> 00:39:33.469
paying earning. Now in other words allowing banks to pay

00:39:33.489 --> 00:39:38.610
dividends was extremely offensive to some of us. That's it,

00:39:38.630 --> 00:39:42.500
you can see the paper and some other writings in this

00:39:42.519 --> 00:39:47.230
website. We had copies of some things out there. And I guess

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:51.659
we will answer questions. I am the last so, but any one will

00:39:51.679 --> 00:39:53.109
answer question if there are any.

00:39:53.109 --> 00:39:54.109
[Informal Talk]

00:39:54.109 --> 00:40:09.230
Question: Could you elaborate on the point of having a ratio

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:13.489
that's completely risk insensitive because I see a problem

00:40:13.509 --> 00:40:17.239
both ways? Basel I was risk insensitive and it led to

00:40:17.259 --> 00:40:20.809
regulatory arbitrage because if bank was told to hold the

00:40:20.829 --> 00:40:25.259
same capital for a loan to a AAA firm as to anon-investment

00:40:25.279 --> 00:40:29.900
grade firm it led to all sorts of consequences but the

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:33.940
converse is if you do have risk sensitivity your argument

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:38.650
would seem to imply. It doesn't matter if risk measure or

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:41.630
wrong because banks should be indifferent to how much

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:44.900
capital they hold and that seems very paradoxical.

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:50.339
No, no, no, not banks but society. Banks will not like it,

00:40:50.359 --> 00:40:53.190
but I am talking about regulators. With the vision that we

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:57.009
have, the vision that we describe is certainly your company

00:40:57.029 --> 00:40:59.609
you write, I mean capital does not mean anything except for

00:40:59.609 --> 00:41:03.630
the risk. And if you have all treasuries in your assets then

00:41:03.650 --> 00:41:06.639
my capital requirements are not, they don't need to be high.

00:41:06.639 --> 00:41:10.659
So clearly it has to be a function of debt. The image that

00:41:10.679 --> 00:41:14.150
we have on capital requirements is that of a margin

00:41:14.170 --> 00:41:16.500
requirement and exchange. We have in mind this smart

00:41:16.519 --> 00:41:20.599
regulated, instead of what Erwin wants them to do, which is

00:41:20.619 --> 00:41:25.150
to know when that point is, when a complicated bank is

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.420
insolvent and just be there overnight to transform its

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:31.039
balance sheet or when 15 banks are doing that we want the

00:41:31.059 --> 00:41:34.980
regulators in healthy times to monitor just like an exchange

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:39.420
does for marginal cost, and to hold their hands on leverage,

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:42.449
to monitor the systemic risk, more than even the individual

00:41:42.469 --> 00:41:45.809
risk because may be some firms should be allowed to fail and

00:41:45.829 --> 00:41:49.029
then adjust the capital requirements to the risks. So when I

00:41:49.049 --> 00:41:51.799
said 30% I mean like for example relative to current assets

00:41:51.799 --> 00:41:56.000
I don't know. We have to work on that, so I don't know.

00:41:56.019 --> 00:42:10.839
Question: I am with JP. Morgan and bank of our size, if we

00:42:10.859 --> 00:42:15.819
were to follow your prescription round numbers here, we

00:42:15.839 --> 00:42:24.449
would have to raise $350 billion equity. So what my first

00:42:24.469 --> 00:42:28.579
question is, will you come back to New York with me and work

00:42:28.599 --> 00:42:31.769
on the press release. So I booked the meeting with my

00:42:31.789 --> 00:42:33.799
chairman to say that he should issue something.

00:42:33.819 --> 00:42:38.219
Sure, I want all of the banks to do this over time. So you

00:42:38.239 --> 00:42:43.319
should pay the dividend, that's the first thing.

00:42:43.339 --> 00:42:48.369
Question: Okay, I predict the meeting wouldn't go too well.

00:42:48.389 --> 00:42:55.849
Second question, why not go to a 100%, I am not sure I got

00:42:55.869 --> 00:42:58.799
that, should we go all the way to 100%?

00:42:58.819 --> 00:43:02.069
I said I am going to leave all these deposits there, I am

00:43:02.069 --> 00:43:04.819
leaving the function of banking, I just don't want, I want

00:43:04.819 --> 00:43:07.069
to backup, they would have liabilities because liability is

00:43:07.069 --> 00:43:09.819
part of their business. So Apple doesn't have that as part

00:43:09.819 --> 00:43:12.569
of their business, but banks do. I want to backup their

00:43:12.569 --> 00:43:15.539
liabilities more than it's backed up by now, that's what I

00:43:15.539 --> 00:43:18.679
want. I think some of the backing, some of the liabilities

00:43:18.699 --> 00:43:21.719
are not essential to their business, long term debt is not

00:43:21.739 --> 00:43:24.259
essential to banking business, nobody is going to tell me

00:43:24.279 --> 00:43:25.980
why they need long term debt investment-

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:29.719
We are not advocating a 100% equity, clearly that's pushing

00:43:29.739 --> 00:43:32.469
into direction of reductio ad absurdum. What we are doing is

00:43:32.469 --> 00:43:36.159
we are starting right now and we are saying and it's really

00:43:36.179 --> 00:43:39.559
what David Mile said, and we are backing up by just some

00:43:39.579 --> 00:43:42.420
clear headed thinking about it in the sense of, if we push

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:47.230
equity more and it may take a little bit time to get $300

00:43:47.250 --> 00:43:50.000
billion more in equity, we understand that. But if you keep

00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:53.190
equity more, and point that you can do is not said is not

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:58.670
pay dividends for a while, but if you basically push more

00:43:58.690 --> 00:44:02.009
and have a bigger equity question as Peter has explained

00:44:02.029 --> 00:44:04.779
that's not going to change your cost of funding. You may

00:44:04.779 --> 00:44:07.670
believe that. It's only going to change your cost of the

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:10.170
funding to the extent that it takes away tax advantages,

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:12.569
which we really should work to neutralize.

00:44:12.589 --> 00:44:15.980
And I realize a lot of us would like to just say we can't

00:44:15.980 --> 00:44:19.480
bid over the tax code, the tax code is what it is, but that'

00:44:19.480 --> 00:44:22.230
s too easy. In other words, if we are really creating

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:24.819
problems because we are unwilling to change the tax code,

00:44:24.839 --> 00:44:29.269
that's a real problem. So let's basically take away the

00:44:29.289 --> 00:44:33.119
subsidies, take away the tax code, push it into direction of

00:44:33.139 --> 00:44:37.719
having self-insurance, by people out there, it should be

00:44:37.739 --> 00:44:41.319
self-insuring you and me, not through the tax systems but

00:44:41.339 --> 00:44:43.519
through holding equity in banks.

00:44:43.539 --> 00:44:49.019
And go to very comfortable point where we know that we have

00:44:49.039 --> 00:44:53.230
got enough to basically survive the type of thing that we

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:56.299
saw in 2007 and 2008. Your bank did better than others so

00:44:56.319 --> 00:45:00.230
may be it's unfair that you are coming under this, but we

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:04.049
know that some other banks didn't do that well. And we have

00:45:04.069 --> 00:45:09.179
to make it safe for the entire sector and as a first order

00:45:09.199 --> 00:45:13.389
effect having a lot more equity, not going to 100% but

00:45:13.409 --> 00:45:17.380
getting may be 10% or 15%, is going to be a huge benefit and

00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:20.329
the costs are just private cost and we should figure out how

00:45:20.329 --> 00:45:24.250
to basically take those away, so that the right decisions

00:45:24.269 --> 00:45:26.359
get made on an economic basis.

00:45:26.379 --> 00:45:39.500
Question: So Paul, I think it was in one of your slides, it

00:45:39.519 --> 00:45:44.230
talked about kind of manipulation opportunities with Wilson

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:48.079
solution, and Wilson when you were talking, I kept thinking

00:45:48.099 --> 00:45:51.099
and I am trying to think in my mind, what's the transition

00:45:51.099 --> 00:45:54.059
between debt holders and equity holders because you are

00:45:54.079 --> 00:45:56.579
probably aren't going to have a transition. And I think

00:45:56.579 --> 00:45:59.389
there is something that's important about both of these,

00:45:59.409 --> 00:46:02.449
there is friction in each one. There is also a lot of

00:46:02.469 --> 00:46:04.969
friction in what you are proposing I think probably more.

00:46:04.969 --> 00:46:09.480
Could the two of you just comment on that friction concept

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:11.699
more?

00:46:11.719 --> 00:46:15.569
I think our position is yet, we don't see what these

00:46:15.589 --> 00:46:19.569
frictions are, in terms of true economic costs. Again there

00:46:19.589 --> 00:46:23.420
are private issues here that a lot of people in this room

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:26.589
focus on but remove those and think about what the true

00:46:26.609 --> 00:46:30.789
social costs are and those aren't salient. I think our

00:46:30.809 --> 00:46:35.849
reservation about any system that requires some individual

00:46:35.869 --> 00:46:41.980
with some discretion in a position of distress to pull some

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:46.029
triggers, that's something that makes you wonder why haven't

00:46:46.049 --> 00:46:49.179
you done that, why not just have that as equity in the first

00:46:49.179 --> 00:46:52.859
place, that in particular applies to the cocos. So it may be

00:46:52.879 --> 00:46:56.099
good to have a lot of equity and then in addition have bail-

00:46:56.099 --> 00:47:02.170
in, but again if you are having liabilities out there that

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:04.639
are going to provide that bail-in there is still the

00:47:04.639 --> 00:47:07.989
question, what are they serving, why can't they be equity. I

00:47:08.009 --> 00:47:11.569
think it's a good question. And they are not cheaper. That's

00:47:11.589 --> 00:47:14.289
the point they are not cheaper, there is the illusion of

00:47:14.289 --> 00:47:17.289
them being cheaper because you say I am only paying 6% on

00:47:17.289 --> 00:47:20.539
this and my equity is costing me 15% but we know that that's

00:47:20.539 --> 00:47:21.539
going to be correct.

00:47:21.539 --> 00:47:24.289
Yeah, what let me do the friction just to clarify is,

00:47:24.289 --> 00:47:28.179
probably when you put up the thing about the reach to 30%, I

00:47:28.199 --> 00:47:30.199
didn't know fair amount about that market and how it got the

00:47:30.199 --> 00:47:33.259
way it is, and I think as far as functionally analysts look

00:47:33.279 --> 00:47:35.629
at things. And I think analysts have a way of looking at

00:47:35.629 --> 00:47:38.960
things today, both on the sell side and buy side and the

00:47:38.980 --> 00:47:43.119
business is making that transition is the friction I think

00:47:43.139 --> 00:47:45.900
that's associated with your kind of concept. Now the

00:47:45.920 --> 00:47:48.889
friction in Wilson's is quite different, it's literally a

00:47:48.909 --> 00:47:52.559
movement in money between a class of owners that won't be

00:47:52.579 --> 00:47:55.119
able to continue holding something because their own capital

00:47:55.139 --> 00:47:58.139
requirements will go up. So you have to move the money over

00:47:58.139 --> 00:48:01.389
to a different set of buyers. And so I just wanted to hear

00:48:01.400 --> 00:48:03.900
about how that would work and particularly also, I didn't

00:48:03.900 --> 00:48:05.949
understand Wilson to have a notion of a trigger that had to

00:48:05.949 --> 00:48:13.359
pulled, it just happens. Yes.

00:48:13.379 --> 00:48:14.379
[Audience]

00:48:14.379 --> 00:48:18.639
We didn't get into the exact issues of triggers. There has

00:48:18.659 --> 00:48:21.940
been a lot of discussions. I think the easiest one for me is

00:48:21.960 --> 00:48:25.009
one of the banks is either critically undercapitalized or

00:48:25.029 --> 00:48:28.509
critically illiquid. And that can be in a bailing system

00:48:28.529 --> 00:48:31.779
late, it can be on even Friday when they tell you we can't

00:48:31.779 --> 00:48:35.079
settle trades on Monday. That does not require a lot of

00:48:35.099 --> 00:48:37.409
discretion that requires knowledge of the fact of deep

00:48:37.429 --> 00:48:42.469
illiquidity. In a lot of the systems we use with the FDIC,

00:48:42.489 --> 00:48:45.670
we can move a little bit earlier than that. So there are

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:48.440
triggers that sometimes happen in advance of that, but it

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:53.909
still should be a very laid in the game debt like event. But

00:48:53.929 --> 00:48:57.799
that happens already with respect to FDIC institutions. And

00:48:57.819 --> 00:49:00.170
I think the bond market is kind of comfortable saying this

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:03.980
is what default consultancy means for a bank. You could move

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:08.259
that further back if you didn't have FDIC funds at-risk to

00:49:08.279 --> 00:49:11.839
something where you couldn't fund on Monday. To answer your

00:49:11.859 --> 00:49:16.639
more general question, at least as I see it I am not an

00:49:16.659 --> 00:49:19.900
economist like the few people up here. The transition from

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:23.880
where we are to a bailing system relies on the capital

00:49:23.900 --> 00:49:28.440
structured banking as it largely exists today. I think that

00:49:28.460 --> 00:49:32.130
makes things a lot simpler. I think there will be

00:49:32.150 --> 00:49:35.059
transitional cost but I think they are much easier to

00:49:35.079 --> 00:49:39.190
manage. Some increase in debt cost. But I think that's a

00:49:39.210 --> 00:49:42.210
reasonable price worth paying to get too big to fail out. I

00:49:42.210 --> 00:49:44.960
think some of the cost proposed in this system would be

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:48.519
potentially much larger. I know that I know you disagree

00:49:48.539 --> 00:49:49.539
with me on that.

00:49:49.539 --> 00:49:52.289
Question: I want you to tell me the cost of withholding

00:49:52.289 --> 00:49:55.289
equity path that nobody has been able to give me the cost.

00:49:55.289 --> 00:49:59.750
I can give you one example. When we issued our cocos, which

00:49:59.769 --> 00:50:05.929
have to turn in equity on either a capital trigger a failure

00:50:05.949 --> 00:50:08.759
trigger or government assistance trigger so in any one at

00:50:08.779 --> 00:50:11.779
the bad states where they have to become equity. So they are

00:50:11.779 --> 00:50:15.960
not in anyway protected by the state. Issuing that debt

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:18.710
raised the value of our equity by about 10% over the course

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:21.909
of that next week relative to people saying I thought

00:50:21.929 --> 00:50:26.049
creditors might have to attain all of this equity over the

00:50:26.069 --> 00:50:29.929
next period, that this was something that the market seemed

00:50:29.949 --> 00:50:33.690
to value this package of instruments better than just raw

00:50:33.710 --> 00:50:34.710
equity.

00:50:34.710 --> 00:50:38.549
Well your triggers that we need to discuss this later, but

00:50:38.569 --> 00:50:46.299
the fact of the matter is that if the total risk is worn by

00:50:46.319 --> 00:50:48.819
the balance sheet, then we have fundamental insight that the

00:50:48.819 --> 00:50:51.319
risk cannot be held by somebody else. And therefore the

00:50:51.319 --> 00:50:53.569
overall cost cannot change, that's a physical reality of

00:50:53.569 --> 00:50:58.239
this. And this is like a gravitating force. In terms of

00:50:58.259 --> 00:51:03.429
triggers, I think the one of the frictions is captured by

00:51:03.449 --> 00:51:07.839
the statement that was ruled in a recent paper by an author

00:51:07.859 --> 00:51:10.359
that actually was invited to be here today but couldn't,

00:51:10.359 --> 00:51:15.549
that shows that there is extremely if there is a knife-edge

00:51:15.569 --> 00:51:18.279
case, where if you have a price trigger, price equity

00:51:18.299 --> 00:51:22.119
trigger, there even exists sort of a way, rational way to

00:51:22.139 --> 00:51:25.219
price these things because there could be situations where

00:51:25.239 --> 00:51:28.250
if you didn't have the trigger and the conversion ratio just

00:51:28.269 --> 00:51:31.049
right, there would either be a situation where if you assume

00:51:31.069 --> 00:51:35.589
that it converts to dozen and that sort of multiple

00:51:35.609 --> 00:51:37.269
[inaudible] all kinds of things like that.

00:51:37.269 --> 00:51:42.920
The trigger based on book values is not clearly going to

00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:46.170
prevent a crisis. And so we would debate, today and tomorrow

00:51:46.190 --> 00:51:49.429
about these triggers. Our point potentially, our point

00:51:49.449 --> 00:51:53.420
completely is that we have not being given a reason by Erwin

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:56.909
or anybody that whatever he was going to issue as cocos

00:51:56.929 --> 00:51:59.429
should not be equity. Just because you can actually sell

00:51:59.429 --> 00:52:02.699
something does not prove anything, because you can sell any

00:52:02.719 --> 00:52:05.809
security out there. My final question about cocos is, if

00:52:05.829 --> 00:52:10.159
they are so good, why don't firms just issue them of course

00:52:10.179 --> 00:52:13.250
in the high-seed economy, in other words they are not

00:52:13.269 --> 00:52:16.019
illegal to issue. There are lots of things that can be

00:52:16.019 --> 00:52:18.599
issued. If they do something, like get tax benefits and

00:52:18.619 --> 00:52:22.400
remove and reduce bankruptcy cost, why don't we see cocos

00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:25.839
issued in the rest of the economy. Here they are issued

00:52:25.859 --> 00:52:29.049
because they have Swiss capital requirements allow half of

00:52:29.069 --> 00:52:31.670
the capital requirements to be satisfied this way. So there

00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:34.989
instead of equity they are issued but why aren't they issued

00:52:35.009 --> 00:52:37.869
with the rest of the market?

00:52:37.889 --> 00:52:58.129
The rest of the market use Chapter 11.

00:52:58.149 --> 00:52:59.149
[Audience]

00:52:59.149 --> 00:53:14.670
But having spent sometime with leverage finance people, you

00:53:14.690 --> 00:53:18.879
get very coco like effects. With regular debt structures and

00:53:18.899 --> 00:53:21.849
industrial corporations you don't get that for financials.

00:53:21.869 --> 00:53:26.049
So we have a lot of this automatic equitization in a normal

00:53:26.069 --> 00:53:29.369
industrial company that doesn't happen naturally for banks.

00:53:29.389 --> 00:53:31.159
So we would do something different.

00:53:31.179 --> 00:53:34.179
Because the banks don't want to, but I am saying that they

00:53:34.179 --> 00:53:37.299
will feel differently about it if regulators make them and

00:53:37.319 --> 00:53:43.909
if they are already highly -

00:53:43.929 --> 00:53:51.750
Question: So first to comment, it's interesting I guess that

00:53:51.769 --> 00:53:55.549
I noticed sort of two themes in the discussion. One is the

00:53:55.569 --> 00:53:59.159
externalities of bank failure are huge. And so they are huge

00:53:59.179 --> 00:54:03.750
imperfections in the asset market which arise when banks

00:54:03.769 --> 00:54:06.549
fail. At the same time you have more

00:54:06.569 --> 00:54:08.449
[inaudible] requires very, very good capital markets and

00:54:08.449 --> 00:54:11.569
very wise investors, to perfectly see through that the risk

00:54:11.589 --> 00:54:15.569
of their holdings has fallen in a certain way. So it's

00:54:15.589 --> 00:54:18.339
interesting to me you have both of those concepts going at

00:54:18.339 --> 00:54:22.710
the same time, just a comment. I guess my second question is

00:54:22.730 --> 00:54:25.920
this, why didn't the banking sector work better in the

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:34.829
1800s. Capital was 30%, 40%. It was in the UK it was in the

00:54:34.849 --> 00:54:38.989
US. People still made lots of bad decisions, I think there

00:54:39.009 --> 00:54:42.199
were still railroad bubbles, canal bubbles, this or that

00:54:42.219 --> 00:54:47.650
etc. You had lots of economic history written on this period

00:54:47.670 --> 00:54:52.009
and thus sort of bad -- of banking why did that happen.

00:54:52.029 --> 00:54:54.699
I am actually not a historian of banks, I barely know

00:54:54.699 --> 00:55:02.869
anything about banks. But I am asking my questions and I

00:55:02.889 --> 00:55:05.889
still didn't give him any answers so I know enough to ask

00:55:05.889 --> 00:55:08.889
good questions from the year and half I am looking at it.

00:55:08.889 --> 00:55:12.389
But the history of bank I don't like to know, but I can say

00:55:12.389 --> 00:55:15.980
the following. These banks were not publicly held, the

00:55:16.000 --> 00:55:19.509
access to equity could have been, they didn't have

00:55:19.529 --> 00:55:21.949
diversification opportunities like banks today have. The

00:55:21.969 --> 00:55:25.139
world is different. The question is why can't the world be

00:55:25.159 --> 00:55:28.049
the same on the total capitalization, that's the question,

00:55:28.069 --> 00:55:31.819
it's because our banks right now why not. So we can go back

00:55:31.839 --> 00:55:34.839
in history and ask these questions and we can come up with

00:55:34.839 --> 00:55:38.089
all kinds of reasons that might or might not have to do with

00:55:38.089 --> 00:55:41.339
where we are right now. But what we are asking is right now

00:55:41.339 --> 00:55:44.339
for the banking system, they we have right now and given the

00:55:44.339 --> 00:55:47.339
crisis that we had just recently, why can't we, what is the

00:55:47.339 --> 00:55:49.339
impediment for banks to stopping dividends and equity

00:55:49.339 --> 00:55:51.839
payouts and raise some right offerings and other things and

00:55:51.839 --> 00:55:54.339
capitalize themselves over the next five years, six years to

00:55:54.339 --> 00:56:06.629
level that seem crisis but may be or not.

00:56:06.649 --> 00:56:07.649
